 101,685 8,539 7.5 62,839\nL61@P0: 1985. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 178,206 115,461 64.8 107,150 60.1 3,179 103,971 8,312 7.2 62,744\nL62@P0: 1986. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 180,587 117,834 65.3 109,597 60.7 3,163 106,434 8,237 7.0 62,752\nL63@P0: 1987. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 182,753 119,865 65.6 112,440 61.5 3,208 109,232 7,425 6.2 62,888\nL64@P0: 1988. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 184,613 121,669 65.9 114,968 62.3 3,169 111,800 6,701 5.5 62,944\nL65@P0: 1989. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 186,393 123,869 66.5 117,342 63.0 3,199 114,142 6,528 5.3 62,523\nL66@P0: 1990. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 189,164 125,840 66.5 118,793 62.8 3,223 115,570 7,047 5.6 63,324\nL67@P0: 1991. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 190,925 126,346 66.2 117,718 61.7 3,269 114,449 8,628 6.8 64,578\nL68@P0: 1992. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 192,805 128,105 66.4 118,492 61.5 3,247 115,245 9,613 7.5 64,700\nL69@P0: 1993. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 194,838 129,200 66.3 120,259 61.7 3,115 117,144 8,940 6.9 65,638\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n20-7028 Mast v. Fillmore County (07/02/2021) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/20-7028_o758.pdf)\nciteturn64view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/20-7028_o758.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 221\nL0@P0: Cite as: 594 U. S. ____ (2021) 1\nL1@P0: ALITO, J., concurring in judgment\nL2@P0: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL3@P0: AMOS MAST, ET AL. v. FILLMORE COUNTY,\nL4@P0: MINNESOTA, ET AL.\nL5@P0: ON PETITION FOR WRIT OF CERTIORARI TO THE COURT\nL6@P0: OF APPEALS OF MINNESOTA\nL7@P0: No. 20–7028. Decided July 2, 2021\nL8@P0: The motion of petitioners for leave to proceed in forma\nL9@P0: pauperis and the petition for a writ of certiorari are\nL10@P0: granted. The judgment is vacated, and the case is re\u0002manded to the Court of Appeals of Minnesota for further\nL11@P0: consideration in light of Fulton v. Philadelphia, 593 U. S.\nL12@P0: ___ (2021).\nL13@P0:  JUSTICE ALITO, concurring in the judgment.\nL14@P0: I agree that we should vacate the judgment below and\nL15@P0: remand for further consideration. The lower court plainly\nL16@P0: misinterpreted and misapplied the Religious Land Use and\nL17@P0-1: Institutionalized Persons Act. Cite as: 594 U. S. ____ (2021) 1\nL18@P1: GORSUCH, J., concurring\nL19@P1: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL20@P1: AMOS MAST, ET AL. v. FILLMORE COUNTY,\nL21@P1: MINNESOTA, ET AL.\nL22@P1: ON PETITION FOR WRIT OF CERTIORARI TO THE COURT\nL23@P1: OF APPEALS OF MINNESOTA\nL24@P1: No. 20–7028. Decided July 2, 2021\nL25@P1:  JUSTICE GORSUCH, concurring in the decision to grant,\nL26@P1: vacate, and remand.\nL27@P1: The Swartzentruber Amish are religiously committed to\nL28@P1: living separately from the modern world. Maintaining that\nL29@P1: commitment is not easy. They grow their own food, tend\nL30@P1: their farms using pre-industrial equipment, and make their\nL31@P1: own clothes. In short, they lead lives of faith and self-reli\u0002ance that have “not altered in fundamentals for centuries.”\nL32@P1: Wisconsin v. Yoder, 406 U. S. 205, 216–217 (1972).\nL33@P1: In this long-running litigation, officials in Fillmore\nL34@P1: County, Minnesota have insisted that the Amish must\nL35@P1: adopt certain modern technologies or risk jail, fines, and\nL36@P1: even losing their farms. Today, the Court grants the\nL37@P1: Amish’s petition for review, vacates the lower court’s deci\u0002sions, and remands this case for further proceedings in light\nL38@P1: of our recent decision in Fulton v. Philadelphia, 593 U. S.\nL39@P1: ___ (2021). I support this decision and write to highlight a\nL40@P1: few issues the lower courts and administrative authorities\nL41@P1: may wish to consider on remand.\nL42@P1: *\nL43@P1: Each Amish community has its own body of religious\nL44@P1: rules, called an Ordnung. When a community must decide\nL45@P1: whether its faith permits a certain action—say, using the\nL46@P1: phone at a neighbor’s farm should a fire break out—it\nL47@P1-2: makes that decision collectively. Sometimes there are dis\u0002agreements and communities fracture. Over time, this phe\u0002nomenon has led to approximately 40 different affiliations\nL48@P2: 2 MAST v. FILLMORE COUNTY\nL49@P2: GORSUCH, J., concurring\nL50@P2: within the broader Amish community across the United\nL51@P2: States. The Swartzentruber Amish are among the most\nL52@P2: traditional.\nL53@P2: Today’s dispute is about plumbing, specifically the dis\u0002posal of gray water—water used in dishwashing, laundry,\nL54@P2: and the like. The Swartzentruber Amish do not have run\u0002ning water in their homes, at least as most would under\u0002stand it. Water arrives through a single line and is either\nL55@P2: pumped by hand or delivered by gravity from an external\nL56@P2: cistern.\nL57@P2: In 2013, Fillmore County adopted an ordinance requiring\nL58@P2: most homes to have a modern septic system for the disposal\nL59@P2: of gray water. See App. to Pet. for Cert. 3; Minn. Stat.\nL60@P2: §115.55(2)(a) (2020); Minn. Admin. Rules 7080.1050–\nL61@P2: 7080.2550 (2019); Fillmore County, Sub-Surface Sewage\nL62@P2: Treatment System Ordinance §§501–502 (2013). Respond\u0002ing to this development, the Swartzentruber Amish submit\u0002ted a letter explaining that their religion forbids the use of\nL63@P2: such technology and “‘asking in the name of our Lord to be\nL64@P2: exempt’” from the new rule. App. to Brief in Opposition for\nL65@P2: Respondent Minnesota Pollution Control Agency 78 (MPCA\nL66@P2: App.). Instead of accommodating this request or devising a\nL67@P2: solution that respected the Amish’s faith, the Minnesota\nL68@P2: Pollution Control Agency filed an administrative enforce\u0002ment action against 23 Amish families in Fillmore County\nL69@P2: demanding the installation of modern septic systems under\nL70@P2: pain of criminal penalties and civil fines. Id., at 79.\nL71@P2: Faced with this action, the Amish filed their own declar\u0002atory judgment suit in state court against Fillmore County\nL72@P2: and the Minnesota Pollution Control Agency (collectively,\nL73@P2: the County), alleging that the County’s septic-system man\u0002date violated the federal Religious Land Use and Institu\u0002tionalized Persons Act (RLUIPA). But the Amish also of\u0002fered an alternative. They offered to install systems that\nL74@P2: clean gray water in large earthen basins filled with wood\nL75@P2-3: chips that filter water as it drains. These wood chip basins\nL76@P3: Cite as: 594 U. S. ____ (2021) 3\nL77@P3: GORSUCH, J., concurring\nL78@P3: may be more primitive than modern septic systems, but\nL79@P3: other jurisdictions permit their use for the disposal of gray\nL80@P3: water. See App. to Pet. for Cert. 4; id., at 73–74.\nL81@P3: Evidently, none of this pleased the authorities. The\nL82@P3: County replied by filing a counterclaim seeking an order\nL83@P3: displacing the Amish from their homes, removing all their\nL84@P3: possessions, and declaring their homes uninhabitable if the\nL85@P3: Amish did not install septic systems within six months.\nL86@P3: MPCA App. 80. The County even unsuccessfully sought a\nL87@P3: court order authorizing its agents to inspect the inside of\nL88@P3: Amish homes as part of an investigation into what “types of\nL89@P3: modern technologies and materials” they might be using.\nL90@P3: Id., at 81. Apparently, this was part of an effort to amass\nL91@P3: “evidence” to “attack the sincerity of [the Amish’s] religious\nL92@P3: beliefs.” Ibid., n. 5.\nL93@P3: Eventually, the case proceeded to trial. There, the state\nL94@P3: trial court rejected the County’s most aggressive argu\u0002ments, including (1) its claim that the Amish’s “limited use\nL95@P3: of telephones” proved that their objection to modern septic\nL96@P3: systems was contrived, App. to Pet. for Cert. 43; (2) its ar\u0002gument that the Bible commands the Amish to submit to\nL97@P3: “secular authority,” id., at 44, n. 16; and (3) its assertion\nL98@P3: that installing septic systems represented only a de mini\u0002mis burden on the Amish’s religious beliefs because they\nL99@P3: sometimes “use various items of ‘modern’ technology,” such\nL100@P3: as “some rubber tires” or “power tools,” id., at 50. At the\nL101@P3: same time, however, the trial court sided with the County\nL102@P3: on the merits, requiring the Amish to install modern septic\nL103@P3: systems. The Minnesota Court of Appeals affirmed and the\nL104@P3: Minnesota Supreme Court denied review. Id., at 2, 76.\nL105@P3: *\nL106@P3: Fulton makes clear that the County and courts below\nL107@P3: misapprehended RLUIPA’s demands. That statute re\u0002quires the application of “strict scrutiny.” Under that form\nL108@P3-4: of review, the government bears the burden of proving both\nL109@P4: \nL110@P4: \nL111@P4: 4 MAST v. FILLMORE COUNTY\nL112@P4: GORSUCH, J., concurring\nL113@P4: that its regulations serve a “compelling” governmental in\u0002terest—and that its regulations are “narrowly tailored.”\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2013 Sequestration: Selected Federal Agencies Reduced Some Services and Investments, While Taking Short-Term Actions to Mitigate Effects | U.S. GAO (https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-452)\nciteturn64view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-452\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 95\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: # 2013 Sequestration: Selected Federal Agencies Reduced Some Services and Investments, While Taking Short-Term Actions to Mitigate Effects\nL3: \nL4: GAO-14-452 Published: May 28, 2014. Publicly Released: May 28, 2014.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Jump To:】 Jump To\nL7: \nL8: ## Highlights\nL9: ## What GAO Found\nL10: \nL11: Fiscal year 2013 sequestration reduced funding to selected components of federal agencies and their program partners—such as state and local governments—that assist in carrying out agency missions. As a result, the selected components and their partners reduced or delayed some services to the public and operations in 2013. For example:\nL12: Public housing authorities reported providing rental assistance to about 41,000 fewer very low-income households compared to 2012—a 2.2 percent reduction—because the authorities received less program funding from the Department of Housing and Urban Development's Office of Public and Indian Housing (PIH).\nL13: The Department of Health and Human Services' Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) reported reducing the frequency of surveys to determine quality of care and compliance with federal standards at psychiatric hospitals from once every three years to once every four to five years and specialized organ transplant centers from once every three years to once every four to six years.\nL14: The Department of Homeland Security's U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) reported that sequestration reductions did not leave them with sufficient funds to provide the overtime necessary to fully staff inspection booths which resulted in increased average wait times for international passengers. From fiscal years 2012 to 2013, wait times increased from 19.7 minutes to 22.8 minutes at one airport and from 20.9 minutes to 26.8 minutes at another.\nL15: School districts GAO spoke with reported that reduced funding from the Department of Education's Office of Elementary and Secondary Education (OESE) resulted in less resources for specialists providing extra instruction to students and an increase in the average number of students per elementary school class. For example, one district reported an average increase of two or three students per elementary school class from the prior school year.\nL16: The Department of Defense (DOD) reported that canceled or limited military training and readiness activities could increase the number of nondeployable units, decrease surge capacity to meet additional requirements with ready forces, and lead to skills gaps.\nL17: In some cases, program partners reported increasing reliance on other federal, state, and local funding sources, where available, to mitigate sequestration's effects on their services to the public. Certain state agencies reported that they relied on additional state funds to inspect and investigate health care facilities on behalf of CMS. However, in other cases, program partners reported that reductions in other funding sources magnified sequestration's effects on services to the public.\nL18: For example, officials at 7 school districts that receive federal education grants reported that sequestration compounded prior-year reductions in state funding.\nL19: However, GAO found in some cases the effects of sequestration could not be isolated from the effects of other changes in funding. For example:\nL20: \nL21: The effects of the 2 percent sequestration of Medicare payments are difficult to quantify due to the challenge of isolating the effects of sequestration from other factors that increased or decreased payments to providers, as well as possible changes in provider behavior to compensate for the sequestration reductions.\nL22: It is difficult to isolate the specific effects of sequestration on Title I school districts because Title I funding typically makes up a small portion of the school district's total funding compared to state and local sources. A district's Title I formula allocation could also be reduced as a result of factors other than sequestration.\nL23: GAO found that sequestration planning and implementation varied among the selected components. In some cases, agencies directed components' efforts, while in others components provided guidance and set priorities for program offices.\nL24: Consistent with GAO's March 2014 report, officials from all federal agency components reported that uncertainty regarding the timing and amount of sequestration and technical questions about how to apply sequestration to certain complex accounts presented challenges for planning and implementation. For example:\nL25: CMS officials had difficulty determining all of the types of provider payments that would need to be cut and which funding was subject to special sequestration rules.\nL26: \nL27: According to CBP budget officials, applying sequestration to fee-based accounts was more difficult than applying sequestration to other accounts.\nL28: In addition, uncertainty surrounding the timing and amount of sequestration limited some components' ability to substantively communicate with program partners and recipients, making it difficult for partners to plan and execute their budgets during the fiscal year. For example, recipients of Indian Housing Block grants from PIH did not receive the full amount of funds until several months into the calendar year.\nL29: Components that had initiated efficiency efforts prior to sequestration reported that they were better positioned to plan for and implement sequestration in fiscal year 2013. For example, CMS officials reported that savings from a 2011 initiative to improve the efficiency of its facility inspections helped the component plan for and implement sequestration.\nL30: In reviewing how agencies implemented sequestration, GAO also selected five programs, projects, and activities (PPAs) from each of the four nondefense case study components based on the specific programs or activities reviewed within each component and other factors and found that components complied with the provision in the Balanced Budget and Emergency Deficit Control Act of 1985 that specifies how PPAs should be identified for the purpose of sequestration.\nL31: In cases where officials had some discretion in implementing sequestration reductions, components reported that they sought to protect higher priority activities, either by using funding flexibilities or modifying or canceling contracts or other ongoing activities. For example, within DOD, the military services sought to protect training requirements for deployed and next-to-deploy forces by canceling or limiting training for forces not preparing to deploy in fiscal year 2014.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn64view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jtfb.southcom.mil/Media/News/Display/Article/4281276/panamax-alpha-2025-us-southern-command-leads-bilateral-exercise-to-protect-pana/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.jtfb.southcom.mil/Media/News/Display/Article/4281276/panamax-alpha-2025-us-southern-command-leads-bilateral-exercise-to-protect-pana/: (403) Forbidden"
